Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||10-05-2001||09:47 PM||||Well I got myself a block heater.  Just weondering if it was the right one.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blockheater.jpg"> <P>Anyway I'm not even sure where to start.  The Haynes manual doesn't tell me how to do it.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_2.JPG"> </A>  <BR>'84 2m4, 4 speed, 106k miles (157k km)  AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||5
Z||000001||samoht||10-05-2001||10:00 PM||||why would you waste your money on a block heater? .. Do you live in Alaska?    The sell ones at Auto zone that you just stick in you dip stick||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ferrari_cdn||10-05-2001||10:05 PM||||These aren't expensive, really.  And no I don't live in Alaska, but in Manitoba.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-05-2001||11:37 PM||||That thing replaces a freeze plug in the motor. Unless you can find a freeze plug of the correct size, it's not happening. To install it, you drain the coolant, knock out the plug ant install the heater according to the directions that should have come with it. It's not rocket science but if you don't do it right the cord gets eaten/melted by the engine or you end up with a coolant leak.<P>The freeze plug could be any of several places on the block.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroBUZZ||10-06-2001||07:32 AM||||Sorry, my car is a v6 so the pics won't help much.  I would use one of the magnetic ones. They stick onto the oil pan. Or perhaps the down the dipstick model.  The one you have does replace a frost plug and Ogre is right on.<BR>Gary<BR>Alaska, LOL!||||reg||1
